WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.709
A pool holds ten loans, and three of them are

00:02.809 --> 00:06.143
going to default. An auditor picks two loans at random.

00:06.243 --> 00:08.703
What's the chance that both are bad ones?

00:10.800 --> 00:14.699
Two laws answer questions like this: one for 'and', one

00:14.799 --> 00:17.753
for 'or'. Plus a trick for 'at least one'.

00:18.850 --> 00:21.447
First pick: three of the ten loans are bad, so

00:21.547 --> 00:24.261
the chance the first one is bad is three tenths.

00:24.583 --> 00:26.551
Picture it as area: the first pick

00:26.651 --> 00:28.741
is bad in this thirty percent slice.

00:28.833 --> 00:31.966
Now, given that the first was bad, two bad loans

00:32.066 --> 00:35.738
remain among nine. So inside that slice, only two ninths

00:35.838 --> 00:38.163
of it has a bad second pick as well.

00:38.250 --> 00:42.298
The chance of both is a fraction of a fraction: three tenths

00:42.398 --> 00:47.484
times two ninths, one in fifteen. That's the multiplicative law. The second

00:47.584 --> 00:52.325
factor is conditional, because the first pick changed the pool. If the

00:52.425 --> 00:56.958
events were independent, it would just be the ordinary probability.

00:58.050 --> 01:01.454
Now 'or'. Sixty percent of a bank's customers have

01:01.554 --> 01:05.310
its credit card, thirty percent have its mortgage. What

01:05.410 --> 01:07.693
share has at least one of the two?

01:08.783 --> 01:12.033
Adding gives ninety percent. But look at the overlap.

01:12.133 --> 01:15.383
Customers with both products were counted once in the

01:15.483 --> 01:17.721
first circle and again in the second.

01:19.316 --> 01:22.711
So subtract the overlap once: sixty plus thirty minus

01:22.811 --> 01:26.997
twenty, seventy percent. That's the additive law. The subtraction

01:27.097 --> 01:30.161
isn't a fudge; it just repairs the double count.

01:31.750 --> 01:35.385
Finally, 'at least one'. A payment system runs on ten independent

01:35.485 --> 01:38.776
servers, and each one fails on a given day with probability

01:38.876 --> 01:41.707
five percent. What's the chance at least one fails?

01:41.800 --> 01:46.378
'At least one' is a mess: exactly one, or exactly two, or exactly

01:46.478 --> 01:51.272
three, and so on. Its opposite is clean: none fail. And none failing

01:51.372 --> 01:55.518
is an 'and' of ten independent events, so we just multiply.

01:55.616 --> 01:59.804
After ten servers, the chance nothing fails is point nine

01:59.904 --> 02:03.791
five to the tenth, about sixty percent. So the chance

02:03.891 --> 02:07.402
that at least one fails is one minus that: about

02:07.502 --> 02:11.164
forty percent. Small risks, repeated, add up fast.

02:12.250 --> 02:15.681
So: for 'and', multiply, zooming in as you go.

02:15.781 --> 02:19.596
For 'or', add, but subtract what you counted twice.

02:19.696 --> 02:23.434
And for 'at least one', go through the complement.
